// Australian Food Composition Database (AFCD) — a free, government-
// published reference dataset (Food Standards Australia New Zealand,
// Release 3, Dec 2025; CC BY 2.5 AU — https://www.foodstandards.gov.au/
// science-data/food-nutrient-databases/afcd), used here as a fallback
// generic-food source for the "Add to [meal]" search, NOT a replacement
// for the app's own hand-curated FOOD_DATABASE or client-confirmed
// customFoods. It's queried only when neither of those two already-
// verified sources has a match (see allFoods/matchedFoods in
// ClientApp.jsx) — exactly the "check APEX's existing verified food
// database first" rule this whole feature is built around.
//
// Every entry below was built once, offline, from the real AFCD Release 3
// "Food details" + "Nutrient profiles (per 100 g)" files: joined on
// Public Food Key, restricted to genuinely generic whole-food groups
// (cereals/grains, fats & oils, fish & seafood, fruit, eggs, meat &
// poultry & game, milk products, dairy/meat alternatives, seeds & nuts,
// vegetables, legumes & pulses — explicitly excluding beverages, cereal-
// based "dishes", sauces/condiments, snack foods, confectionery, alcohol
// and composite recipes, which either overlap what FOOD_DATABASE/
// fitnessMealsAU.js already cover or aren't "generic" in the sense this
// app needs), restricted to 'Analysed'/'Borrowed' derivation (the two
// most directly sourced categories, never 'Imputed'/'Estimated'), and
// name-filtered to exclude anything reading as a prepared dish or recipe
// rather than a single generic food. Energy was converted from AFCD's
// native kJ to kcal (÷4.184) and every row was checked against the same
// Atwater energy-reconciliation math validateNutrition() (barcodeLookup.js)
// uses on a live barcode scan — any row that failed it was left out
// entirely rather than shipped as a flagged static entry. `liquid: true`
// is set explicitly (never guessed) on the handful of entries whose AFCD
// name wouldn't otherwise be recognised as a drink by foodDatabase.js's
// isLiquidFood() name heuristic (plant-milk "beverage" products) — this
// is exactly the "drinks logged in grams instead of mL" failure mode
// flagged in the task this file was built for; everything else relies on
// that same shared, already-audited classifier, same as every other food
// in this app.
//
// `source: "AFCD"` and `afcdKey` (the AFCD Public Food Key) are carried
// on every entry so a client's own label-verified correction (saved via
// FoodQuantitySheet's "Enter the correct numbers from the label" flow —
// see buildCorrectedFood in barcodeLookup.js, reused as-is here) can be
// told apart from the original bundled figure, and so a corrected
// customFoods doc naturally outranks this file's entry on a future search
// (plain name-based precedence — see allFoods in ClientApp.jsx).
//
// This is a deliberately small, practical subset (175 of AFCD's ~1,600
// published foods) — not a wholesale import. It exists to fill real gaps
// in FOOD_DATABASE (exotic/less common proteins, specific cheeses and
// fish species, wholefood grains, fresh herbs, etc.), not to duplicate
// the ~1,300 generic foods FOOD_DATABASE already has under simpler,
// consumer-facing names.
